# Copy to `.env`, fill in, `chmod 600`. `.env` never ships.
#
# The launcher merges this into a rendered config at launch, so the key never
# has to be written into `config.json` (which does ship).

# The model this agent is tuned for. Leave PPT_API_KEY blank and the launcher
# falls back to the host raven's provider block instead - which brings that
# raven's own model along with its credentials, so the two lines below are read
# only when this folder has a key of its own. See README.
#
# These three drive more than the endpoint they name:
#
# - PPT_API_BASE, with the key's own prefix, is what tells raven *which* gateway
#   this is. `config.json` deliberately names its provider block something raven
#   carries no spec for, so nothing shadows that detection - which is what keeps
#   prompt caching on where the endpoint supports it. Point this at a gateway
#   raven does not recognise and it falls back to sending the model id
#   unprefixed; a private OpenAI-compatible endpoint may need the block renamed
#   to `custom` on both lines of `config.json` by hand.
# - PPT_MODEL sizes the context window. The launcher looks the id up in the
#   host's model catalog (`$RAVEN_HOME/cache/model-catalog.json`) and writes that
#   model's real ceiling into `agents.defaults.contextWindowTokens`; a model the
#   catalog does not list leaves the shipped number alone rather than guessing.
# - PPT_API_KEY, besides paying for all of it, is read for its prefix by the
#   detection above, so a key from one gateway with another's address is a
#   mismatch worth checking before blaming the model.
PPT_API_KEY=
PPT_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
PPT_API_BASE=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# Serper key for `web_search` (https://serper.dev). Inherited when blank: the
# launcher reads the host raven's own `tools.web.search.apiKey`, so one rotation
# covers every folder. Fill it in only to bill this agent's searching apart from
# the host's. With neither - and no `SERPER_API_KEY` in the environment this
# launcher is handed, which the runtime reads as a last resort - every
# `web_search` call is refused, and the deck loses its pictures, because that is
# the only tool that finds them.
PPT_SERPER_API_KEY=

# Jina Reader key, inherited from the host's `tools.web.jinaApiKey` the same way.
# `r.jina.ai` is called unauthenticated when no side has one, at a lower rate
# limit. An out-of-credit key answers 402 where no key at all answers 200, so a
# dead key disables page reading entirely - and because blank here means the
# host's key, an exhausted key *on the host* now disables `web_fetch` in this
# folder too, where a blank line used to insulate it from that. There is no
# third setting for "deliberately none": an empty assignment reads as absent,
# so anything written here to opt out becomes a key that gets sent. The remedy
# is to clear the host's key *and* unset `JINA_API_KEY` in the environment the
# launcher is handed - clearing the config alone drops the key out of the
# rendered file and the runtime falls straight through to that variable, so a
# key sitting in both places keeps answering 402. With neither, the anonymous
# path is restored for all four folders at once. Image downloads are unaffected
# either way - `ppt_fetch` fetches them over plain httpx, not through the reader.
PPT_JINA_API_KEY=
